{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"San Diego","level":"city","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"San Diego Residential Tenant Protections (Just Cause for Termination of Tenancy)","requirement":"A landlord may not terminate a tenancy without just cause, which is either at-fault or no-fault. At-fault causes include nonpayment of rent, material lease breach, nuisance, waste, refusal to sign a substantially similar lease renewal, criminal activity or threats, unlawful sublet, refusal of lawful entry, unlawful use, and failure to vacate after termination of employment or after the tenant's own notice. No-fault causes, acted on in good faith, are: owner or relative move-in for at least 12 continuous months (occupancy within 90 days of the tenant vacating); withdrawal from the rental market; compliance with a government or court vacate order or local ordinance; and demolition or substantial remodel. Written notice must state the basis for termination, and curable violations require a prior notice to cure. No-fault terminations require 30 or 60 days' notice under Civ. Code § 1946.1, notice to the San Diego Housing Commission within 3 business days, and relocation assistance paid by direct payment or rent waiver. The tenant has a right of first offer if the unit is re-offered for rent within 5 years after a move-in, government-order or demolition/remodel eviction. Buyout agreements are regulated, retaliation is prohibited, and failure to strictly comply voids the termination notice.","key_value":"Relocation assistance for no-fault termination: 2 months of actual rent, or 3 months if the tenant is a senior (62+) or disabled. It is paid by direct payment within 15 days of the notice or as a rent waiver, regardless of tenant income or length of tenancy.","coverage_conditions":"Applies to residential rental property (any dwelling or unit intended for human habitation, including mobilehome park units) with a tenancy of more than 30 days, except where exempt.","exemptions":"Transient and tourist hotels; short-term residential occupancy; deed-restricted or government-agreement affordable housing and subsidized housing (Section 8 is not exempt); mobilehomes under the Mobilehome Residency Law; nonprofit hospitals, religious facilities, care facilities and nonprofit transitional housing; school dormitories; units where the tenant shares a bathroom or kitchen with a landlord living on-site; an owner-occupied single-family residence renting no more than two bedrooms/ADUs/JADUs; a duplex where the landlord occupies one unit; housing with a certificate of occupancy issued within the previous 15 years (not mobilehomes); and separately alienable property whose landlord is not a REIT, corporation, LLC with a corporate member, or mobilehome park management, provided the tenant received the required written exemption notice.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":{"any":[{"field":"certificate_of_occupancy","op":">","value":{"as_of_minus_years":15}},{"all":[{"field":"property_type","op":"==","value":"single_family"},{"field":"owner_occupied","op":"==","value":true}]},{"all":[{"field":"units","op":"==","value":2},{"field":"owner_occupied","op":"==","value":true}]}]}},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"Consistent with California Civil Code section 1946.2; the rights conferred are in addition to any existing rights provided to tenants by state or federal law. Relocation assistance is credited against any other relocation assistance required by federal, state or other local law.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":"2023-06-24","effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":"2023-05-25","end_date":null,"penalty":"Liability for wrongful eviction of not less than three times actual economic damages; not less than three times required relocation assistance plus actual damages for failure to pay it; punitive damages, injunctive relief and discretionary attorney's fees; the termination notice is void on non-compliance; City civil and criminal enforcement.","citation":"San Diego Municipal Code §§ 98.0701-98.0710 (Ord. O-21647 N.S.; amended by O-21769 N.S.)","quoted_span":"A landlord shall not terminate a tenancy without just cause. For purposes of this Division, just cause includes at-fault just cause and no-fault just cause.","supporting_spans":["(k) housing that has been issued a certificate of occupancy within the previous 15 years, unless the housing is a mobilehome; and","(ii) If the tenant is a senior or disabled, the direct payment to the tenant shall be in an amount equal to three months of actual rent under the tenant’s lease in effect at the date of the notice."],"confidence":0.93,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"The document is the San Diego Municipal Code Chapter 9, Article 8, Division 7. It contains only a just cause eviction rule. The rent cap is mentioned only as a notice reference to Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.12, so it is not recorded as a San Diego rule. Exemptions that depend on non-modeled facts, such as owner type for separately alienable property, are described in the exemptions text only."}
